From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 11:34:29 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: retire a Metal command queue a failure has
 killed

Metal does not revive an MTL4CommandQueue that has killed a submission.
Measured on an A15 with a trace of every commit and every report: after
two genuine GPU timeouts the queue admitted each later commit and
reported it failed in 0.0-0.1 ms, with MTL4CommandQueueErrorTimeout and
an execution interval of a few microseconds, for every submission that
followed. A twelve-case Vulkan CTS reproducer therefore fails its last
seven cases with wrong pixels and wrong buffer contents, and a CTS
fraction fails every remaining case after the collapse.

The queue accepts work and runs none of it, and Metal 4 offers nothing
that resets one, so the driver stops using it. The replacement is
committed to on the queue's own submit thread and only once Metal has
reported every submission the retired queue was given, which keeps this
driver's single-queue ordering intact: no submission is split across the
two queues, and the retired queue holds no report that could arrive
after its owed signals were made good.

Retirement reads a queue's reports and its failure under one lock, so
the commit callback publishes a submission's report and the failure it
carries in the same critical section. Otherwise a failing queue would
look drained and healthy in between, and a later failing submission
could find the earlier one's report already counted, retire the queue,
clear its failure count, and let the first callback fail the queue that
replaced it. The error is described and printed before the lock is
taken so nothing unbounded runs inside it.

The callback also acts on its report inside that critical section: the
sweep a failure owes, the one a completion after a failure owes, and the
drained check with its sweep of everything left. A verdict reached under
the lock and acted on after it could outlive a retirement: the submit
thread could retire the drained queue, clear its failure count and
enqueue signals on the replacement at the serial it last committed, and
the stale sweep would then raise those signals while the replacement was
still running the work they follow. The submit path's drained check
decides and sweeps under one lock for the same reason.

The timeout contract in kk_queue_error.h said a device survives a
timeout because the queue keeps working. The device still survives, but
now because the queue that reported it is retired, and the contract says
so.
